# Exercise 5: Recursion
#
# A rule can use itself. An ancestor is a parent, or a parent of an
# ancestor. That takes two rules with the same name:
#
#   - a base case, which needs nothing more than a fact
#   - a recursive case, which takes one step and then uses the rule again
#
# Sova tries both rules, so writing them as two separate "rule" blocks is
# like joining them with OR.
#
# Your task: write ancestor(a, d), which is true when a is a parent of d,
# a grandparent of d, a great-grandparent, and so on.

fact parent("Ada", "Ben")
fact parent("Ben", "Cy")
fact parent("Cy", "Di")
fact parent("Fay", "Gus")

# Write your rules below this line.



# --- Checks: run ./sova --check on this file. Don't change below here. ---
hint "First rule (base case): a is an ancestor of d if a is a parent of d."
hint "Second rule: a is an ancestor of d if a is a parent of some m, AND m is an ancestor of d."
hint "Put the parent goal before the ancestor goal, so each step gets closer to d."
expect ancestor("Ada", "Ben")
expect ancestor("Ada", "Di")
expect NOT ancestor("Di", "Ada")
expect NOT ancestor("Ada", "Gus")
expect ancestor("Ada", who) gives "Ben", "Cy", "Di"
expect ancestor(who, "Di") gives "Cy", "Ben", "Ada"
